{
  "$schema": "https://turbo.build/schema.json",
  // **Every root script that reports a verdict passes `--continue`, and this is where the reason lives**
  // because `package.json` is strict JSON and cannot hold one (#333).
  //
  // Turbo's default is `--continue=never`: the first failing package cancels the rest. Combined with one
  // intermittently failing package that is not a bug — it is a trap. During the Paddle wave a run came
  // back naming the known `@pithy-sh/storage` timeout, which reads as "one known flake, everything else
  // green." `@pithy-sh/payments:test` had never run. It held all 6,415 insertions of that wave, and
  // nothing in the output said so. **The absence of a result was read as a pass**, which is the gate
  // failure this repository keeps finding from new directions.
  //
  // Reproduced here before it was changed: with one planted failing test, `bun run test` printed
  // `Tasks: 0 successful, 22 total` and `Failed: @pithy-sh/testers#test`. Twenty-one packages produced no
  // result and none of them was named. A reader who recognised the one name learned nothing whatever
  // about the other twenty-one, and had no way to tell that from learning they passed.
  //
  // `always` for `test`, `test:*` and `typecheck`: those tasks declare no `dependsOn`, so every package
  // can run regardless of what failed, and after the change every package has a result.
  //
  // `dependencies-successful` for `build`, because `build` does declare `^build`. A package cannot be
  // built on top of an upstream that did not build, and `always` would bury the one real failure under a
  // wall of derived ones. Cancelled-for-cause is a different thing from silently skipped, and the count
  // still shows it: `successful + failed < total` is the signal, and the failure named is the cause.
  //
  // A developer who wants the old fail-fast behaviour has it a flag away — `--continue=never` on the
  // turbo command. The default belongs to the reader of a verdict, not to the shortest run.
  "tasks": {
    "build": {
      "dependsOn": ["^build"],
      "outputs": ["dist/**"]
    },
    // No `^build` on typecheck or test. Every package exports `./src/*` → `./src/*.ts`, so packages
    // consume each other's TypeScript source and nothing ever reads `dist/`. Depending on `^build`
    // would rebuild every upstream package to produce output no test or typecheck consumes.
    // `build` stays a separate CI step — it proves tsc emit and dts generation still work for publishing.
    //
    // That `^build` was also, by accident, throttling the test run: test tasks started staggered
    // behind their dependencies' builds. Without it every package's vitest launches at once, and the
    // `@cloudflare/vitest-pool-workers` suites each spawn workerd — enough oversubscription to time
    // out `beforeAll` hooks on an 8-core machine. The root `test` script replaces the accidental
    // throttle with an explicit `--concurrency=50%`; keep it there if you touch these deps.
    // `@pithy-sh/vite#build` writes one file outside its own package: `templates/client-env.d.ts` in
    // `@pithy-sh/ui-react`, generated from the four declared client projections (#398). Both halves are
    // named here or the cache lies about it — an output turbo does not know it produced is not restored
    // on a hit, and an input it does not hash is a projection change that replays a stale artifact.
    "@pithy-sh/vite#build": {
      "dependsOn": ["^build"],
      "inputs": ["$TURBO_DEFAULT$", "$TURBO_ROOT$/packages/*/src/client/projection.ts"],
      "outputs": ["dist/**", "$TURBO_ROOT$/packages/ui-react/templates/client-env.d.ts"]
    },
    "typecheck": {},
    // `.github/scripts/` belongs to no workspace, so `turbo run typecheck` never reached either of the
    // two scripts CI's `plan` job runs (#214). They are a fourth program in `packages/cli` now —
    // `tsconfig.ciScripts.json` — because that is where `typescript` and `@types/node` are installed and
    // where the module they import across the tree lives. That leaves one seam: a task's default inputs
    // are the files in its own package, so editing `.github/scripts/planShards.ts` alone would hit a
    // cached `typecheck` and report a pass on source it never compiled. Naming the files closes it.
    // `$TURBO_DEFAULT$` keeps the package's own inputs; `$TURBO_ROOT$` reaches outside it.
    "@pithy-sh/cli#typecheck": {
      "inputs": ["$TURBO_DEFAULT$", "$TURBO_ROOT$/.github/scripts/**/*.ts"]
    },
    // The same seam, one tooling package over, and it swallowed the gate built to close #419 — Jim,
    // 2026-08-21.
    //
    // `tooling/browser-scopes` holds no product code. It exists to prove that a module a browser may
    // import reaches no module that needs the Workers runtime, which is a claim about **every capability
    // in `packages/`** — and a task's default inputs are the files in its own package, so the gate
    // replayed while its entire subject moved underneath it.
    //
    // Reproduced with the branch's cache warm, by planting the exact #419 chain back:
    // `packages/support/src/data/leakD.ts` re-exporting `MAX_LINKED_PURCHASES` from `../link/sender.js`,
    // and one import of it in `packages/support/src/http/responses.ts`. `turbo run test
    // --filter=@pithy-sh/browser-scopes` printed `1 successful, Cached: 1 cached, Time: 16ms >>> FULL
    // TURBO` and replayed the pre-plant logs — `20 passed`, timestamped before the file existed.
    // `typecheck` did the same, `cache hit, replaying logs d30bc0e6bdd6b550`. Forced, the same tree fails
    // and names the chain: `expected [ …(6) ] to deeply equal []`, the first line reading
    // `support/src/http/responses.ts -> support/src/data/leakD.ts -> support/src/link/sender.ts`. CI
    // restores this cache from main, so it is not local-only. A gate that cannot fail on the change it
    // exists to catch is not a weak gate; it is a green tick over unverified work.
    //
    // **The whole of `packages/`, rather than the files the gate reads.** This is the one decision here
    // that is not taken from `#414`'s answer to the same class, and it goes the other way. That lane's
    // fixture has two named packages for a subject, and `tsc --listFiles` says exactly which of their
    // files it opens, so naming those files is both necessary and sufficient. This one derives its
    // expectation from a **directory walk**: `responseCoverage.test.ts` calls
    // `sourcePaths(PACKAGES, …)` to find every `src/http/responses.ts` the kit ships, so that a
    // capability landing tomorrow is covered by the commit that adds it. Its answer therefore changes
    // when a file **appears** — and a file nothing yet imports is a file no compile opens. Keying on what
    // the programs read would leave `packages/newcap/src/http/responses.ts` unhashed, which is precisely
    // the module the coverage assertion exists to demand. `.github/scripts/crossPackageReads.ts` has
    // recorded the same thing all along, from two test files: target `packages`, the directory.
    //
    // `bun.lock` is which copies are on disk. The gate's answer is a list of npm packages the compiler
    // pulled in, so a `bun update` that moved `zod` or `@cloudflare/workers-types` changes what is being
    // classified without a byte of any manifest moving.
    //
    // **This file is in the key too, and it has to be.** A task's hash covers its own definition and no
    // other — measured on turbo 2.10.10 by the `#414` lane. `turboInputs.test.ts` runs in the test task and
    // asserts about all three declarations, so without `turbo.jsonc` named here, narrowing one of them
    // would replay the very test that exists to refuse it.
    //
    // `dependsOn` is not used, and would not do this job. There is no one package to depend on: the
    // subject is every capability, present and future, and `^build` reaches only this package's declared
    // dependencies — which is a list, maintained by hand, of exactly the kind the derived guard exists to
    // refuse. It would also order the run for no reason. The gate needs the capabilities' files, not
    // their verdicts, and every one of those verdicts was green throughout #419.
    //
    // **The three lists are identical, and that is the point rather than a copy-paste.** `test` and
    // `test:node` are one command here — `vitest run`; the package has no workers project — and the
    // guard runs inside it. If the test task were keyed more narrowly than the compile it checks, that
    // edit would replay a green guard over a stale list. One list, checked to be one list.
    //
    // This is the fourth instance of this class found this session, and the second introduced by a fix.
    // `#432` holds the pre-existing half and asks for a shape where the miscoping is impossible rather
    // than corrected. This is the corrected kind, with a test standing over it. When #432 lands a general
    // answer, every entry of this shape should collapse into it.
    "@pithy-sh/browser-scopes#typecheck": {
      "inputs": [
        "$TURBO_DEFAULT$",
        "$TURBO_ROOT$/turbo.jsonc",
        "$TURBO_ROOT$/bun.lock",
        "$TURBO_ROOT$/packages/**",
        "!$TURBO_ROOT$/packages/*/node_modules/**",
        "!$TURBO_ROOT$/packages/*/dist/**",
        "!$TURBO_ROOT$/packages/*/coverage/**",
        "!$TURBO_ROOT$/packages/*/.*/**/*",
        "!$TURBO_ROOT$/packages/cli/templates/**"
      ]
    },
    "@pithy-sh/browser-scopes#test": {
      "inputs": [
        "$TURBO_DEFAULT$",
        "$TURBO_ROOT$/turbo.jsonc",
        "$TURBO_ROOT$/bun.lock",
        "$TURBO_ROOT$/packages/**",
        "!$TURBO_ROOT$/packages/*/node_modules/**",
        "!$TURBO_ROOT$/packages/*/dist/**",
        "!$TURBO_ROOT$/packages/*/coverage/**",
        "!$TURBO_ROOT$/packages/*/.*/**/*",
        "!$TURBO_ROOT$/packages/cli/templates/**"
      ]
    },
    "@pithy-sh/browser-scopes#test:node": {
      "inputs": [
        "$TURBO_DEFAULT$",
        "$TURBO_ROOT$/turbo.jsonc",
        "$TURBO_ROOT$/bun.lock",
        "$TURBO_ROOT$/packages/**",
        "!$TURBO_ROOT$/packages/*/node_modules/**",
        "!$TURBO_ROOT$/packages/*/dist/**",
        "!$TURBO_ROOT$/packages/*/coverage/**",
        "!$TURBO_ROOT$/packages/*/.*/**/*",
        "!$TURBO_ROOT$/packages/cli/templates/**"
      ]
    },
    // `test` runs both vitest projects (node + workers) in one process — the convenient local
    // default, and the one to run before pushing. `test:node` and `test:workers` run one project
    // each, which is what lets CI schedule them separately: the Node-runtime job needs the node
    // suites alone (workerd tests prove nothing about the host runtime that launched them).
    //
    // `--concurrency` is close to irrelevant here, which is worth writing down because it looks
    // like the obvious knob. Measured cold on 8 cores: the full `test` run is 90.2s at 50%, 90.4s
    // at 75%, and 102.4s at 100% — so the only thing tuning buys is a penalty for oversubscribing.
    // 50% stays because it is portable (a share of the machine, not a number for one machine) and
    // because the workers suites each spawn workerd, which is what times out `beforeAll` hooks when
    // too many run at once. The wall clock is set by total CPU work, not by scheduling: cold serial
    // cost is ~191s (node 64s + workers 127s), and 90s is roughly what 8 cores can do with it.
    // The lever that actually works is doing less work — turbo's cache locally, `--affected` in CI.
    "test": {},
    "test:node": {},
    "test:workers": {},
    "test:integration": {
      // Live Cloudflare — never cached; only packages that define the script run it.
      "cache": false
    },
    "lint": {},
    "clean": {
      "cache": false
    },
    "reset": {
      "cache": false
    }
  }
}
